    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d285">

                <pb facs="0557" n="461" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg461"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.512394Shipping/23</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Japanese Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Matsudaira</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-06-08"><hi rendition="#italic">June 8,
                        1926</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Referring further to your note of
                    March 31, 1926, and to previous correspondence in regard to the establishment by
                    the United States and Japan of reciprocal exemption from taxation of income
                    derived from the operation of merchant vessels, I have the honor to inform you
                    of the receipt of a letter on the subject from the Secretary of the Treasury
                    dated May 26, 1926.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d285-f1"/></p>
                <p>The Secretary of the Treasury states that he approved, on February 1, 1926,
                    Treasury Decision 3812 embodying the ruling that from July 18, 1924, Japan
                    satisfies the equivalent exemption provision of Section 213 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) (8) of the Revenue Act of 1924, and that this
                    action is all that is necessary to give effect to the reciprocal arrangement on
                    the part of the United States.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
